#!/usr/bin/env bash
# commit-msg — refuse a commit SUBJECT that makes an unverified quantity claim.
#
# Why this exists, and why it is a hook rather than a line in CLAUDE.md:
# the coordinator wrote a false count into a commit subject TWICE — a test total
# that was arithmetic on remembered figures, and "closes all four Billing
# features" when it closed three. Both were written at the same point in the
# workflow (a session-closing summary), about the same kind of quantity (scale,
# not behaviour), and in both cases the true figure was one command away.
#
# The rule that came out of the first ("do not write a figure you have not read
# off a run in the same session") did not prevent the second, because a rule
# whose only enforcement is the author's memory fires exactly when attention is
# elsewhere. This fires whether or not anyone remembers it.
#
# To satisfy it, put the enumeration in the BODY — the command and its output,
# or an explicit "counted: <what>" line. The subject may then say anything.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, stated because overstating a guard is the failure
# this repository keeps cataloguing: it checks the SUBJECT only. The first of
# the two errors was a figure in the BODY ("508 -> 635"), and this hook would
# not have caught it. That case is different in kind — a body figure is usually
# correct and legitimately present, and what failed there was its PROVENANCE,
# which no regex can see. It is covered by the written rule (do not write a
# figure you have not read off a run in the same session) and, in practice, by
# the reviewer reporting counts from its own runs, which has caught two.
# So: this hook halves the problem mechanically and leaves the other half to
# discipline. Do not read a green hook as "the numbers in this message are true".
set -euo pipefail
MSG_FILE="$1"
SUBJECT="$(head -1 "$MSG_FILE")"
BODY="$(tail -n +2 "$MSG_FILE")"

# A subject making a totality or scale claim.
QUANTIFIER='(\ball\b|\bevery\b|\bboth\b|\bthe last\b|\bcomplete[sd]?\b|\bfinishe[sd]\b|\bnone\b|\bno remaining\b)'
BARE_INTEGER='(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_./-])[0-9]{2,}([^A-Za-z0-9_./%-]|$)'

claims=""
printf '%s' "$SUBJECT" | grep -qiE "$QUANTIFIER" && claims="quantifier"
printf '%s' "$SUBJECT" | grep -qE  "$BARE_INTEGER" && claims="${claims:+$claims, }bare integer"
[ -z "$claims" ] && exit 0

# The body must carry the enumeration that settles it.
if printf '%s' "$BODY" | grep -qiE '^[[:space:]]*(counted|enumerated):' ; then exit 0; fi
if printf '%s' "$BODY" | grep -qE '\$ |^\s{2,}[a-z0-9_.-]+ (-|--)[a-z]' ; then exit 0; fi

cat >&2 <<MSGOUT

commit-msg: the SUBJECT makes a quantity claim ($claims) that the body does not settle.

  subject: $SUBJECT

A claim of completeness is a count, and a count is a reading. This has been wrong
twice in this repository, both times in a commit subject, both times when the true
figure was one command away.

Do one of:
  * remove the quantifier or the number from the subject, or
  * run the enumeration and put it in the body as either
      counted: <what you enumerated, and the result>
    or the command itself with its output.

MSGOUT
exit 1
